.TH CTPOOL_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_shutdown_drain \- graceful shutdown
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_shutdown_drain(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Signals the pool to stop accepting new tasks, waits for all queued
and active tasks to complete, then joins all worker threads. Blocks
until every worker has exited.

After this call returns, no new tasks may be submitted. The pool
struct can then be freed with ctpool_destroy. Calling
ctpool_shutdown_drain a second time is a no-op.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ctpool_submit and ctpool_submit_future return ccol_not_permitted after
this call.
Any thread blocked in ctpool_submit on a full bounded queue is woken
and will receive ccol_not_permitted.

Calling ctpool_shutdown_drain on pool from within a task (or that
task's on_complete callback) currently executing on one of pool's own
worker threads is a complete no-op: it neither stops accepting new
tasks nor joins any worker. A worker thread cannot join itself, so
proceeding could never actually wait for the calling thread to exit,
only for every other worker.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3),
.BR ctpool_wait (3),
.BR ctpool_destroy (3)
